# Anna at the Thanjavur Conference
Tamil title: தஞ்சை மாநாட்டில் அண்ணா

An anti-Hindi conference was held on 25 August 1963. Arignar Anna spoke as follows:

“If the imposition of Hindi were merely a language issue, that would be one thing. But a long history lies behind it. By imposing Hindi, they are using authority to impose autocratic rule upon non-Hindi-speaking people. When we say that we oppose Hindi, it means that we oppose autocratic political domination. It also means that we declare war upon deceivers who seek to introduce linguistic domination through power and supremacy. Our General Council has resolved that Hindi must be opposed. If we do not oppose Hindi, there will be no Tamil consciousness; without Tamil consciousness, no ethnic consciousness as Dravidians will arise; and without that Dravidian consciousness, the conviction that Dravida Nadu belongs to the Dravidians will not emerge.

“When speaking of the freedom struggle conducted by Congress members, they say, ‘He spent six years in Alipore Prison; this man spent seven years,’ and so on. They speak of us from the demeaning assumption that we are incapable of doing anything comparable. Through these conferences, we announce that we are prepared to go to prison for however many years it takes to remove such disparagement.

“As soon as the day of struggle is announced, many thousands of volunteers must stand in formation and compel them to abandon the linguistic domination they intend to institute from 1965.

“We are rapidly holding conferences like this one. At the final conference to be held in Madras, we will announce the day of struggle.

“Hindi is a language that came only yesterday. Tamil was flourishing two thousand years ago. English has been developing for many centuries.

“Today, English is chosen for utility and Tamil for affection. No one has said that Hindi is superior to Tamil.

“Hindi is an inferior language, a language without richness or literature. Scholars who know Hindi themselves say, ‘Only now are we developing Hindi. We are taking words from every language, putting them into the Hindi storehouse, and enriching it.’

“At the same time, those very people say, ‘We do not know Tamil, but we have heard that it is a literary and delightful language.’ What does it mean when they impose impoverished Hindi upon rich Tamil? It means only that they impose it through authority. Great poets, linguistic researchers, and people with administrative experience oppose Hindi. If Hindi scholars may gather and say that it can become the language of government, surely Tamil scholars may gather and say at the very least, ‘Do not impose Hindi upon us.’

“This is not a conference announcing, ‘Let us begin some sort of agitation.’ Following the organisation of the Kazhagam, branch organisations, circle organisations, district organisations, and the General Council assembled and examined the imposition of Hindi. The General Council established a committee for the struggle, appointed Karunanidhi as its chairman, and made Natarajan and other district secretaries members. The resulting declaration is now placed before you. Do you regard opposition to Hindi as your duty? Do you understand that Tamil will be ruined if Hindi arrives? Through conferences such as this, we learn of your renewed feeling, inner strength, spirit, and willingness to sacrifice, and explain the plans you propose.

“The DMK does not wander about searching for agitations. Nor will it remain idle when a situation demanding struggle arises. Some ask why and for what purpose we are conducting this struggle. Others ask, ‘An agitation at a time when the Chinese invasion has created an emergency?’ Those who ask this speak as though they themselves have put on military uniforms, taken up rifles, and are personally confronting every Chinese soldier. I sympathise with them!

“I said that if Chinese aggression produced an emergency for the country, we would attend to that first. On hearing this, some said, ‘Look! Look! They have abandoned their agitation out of fear of the Chinese.’ Others say, ‘As though these people could ever conduct a struggle!’ We do not care about such talk. What matters to us is what our conscience says, what the Kazhagam’s General Council declares, and what our power of thought tells us. Some people in political circles will always speak that way.

“We have waged a long struggle against Hindi. Rajaji was the person who first introduced it. Now that he too opposes it, we unite. When travelling by train, we ask, ‘Has Mayavaram arrived? Has Kumbakonam arrived?’ But Mayavaram and Kumbakonam have not come anywhere; it is the train that has reached them!

“In the same way, the train called the DMK is moving forward. Those who wish may board it. In earlier days, when we opposed Hindi, it was holy men who sat upon the platform.

“Women who participated in the anti-Hindi agitation then would part and comb their hair, fill the entire parting with kumkum, and come to fight. Women with such deep devotion to Saivism participated because they feared that the arrival of Hindi would damage Saivism. College students too opposed Hindi at that time. Everyone was troubled by the fear that the whole of Tamil Nadu would be harmed if Hindi came.

“The present anti-Hindi agitation is the fourth such struggle. Today, if we stumble and fall, we can only fall upon our own MLAs. Kazhagam MLAs, Members of Parliament, municipal councillors, and others are all present to oppose Hindi. Even so, those in Delhi believe that they can use legislation to overcome this power of resistance and impose Hindi. The question is not who will or will not support the anti-Hindi struggle we are about to conduct.

“The DMK General Council resolved to wage a struggle against Hindi. At the Swatantra Party conference held in Salem, a resolution was adopted with great generosity and respect: ‘The Swatantra Party too will participate in the anti-Hindi conference conducted by the DMK.’ The man whom I attacked with harsh words for so long now stands on our side. Whether or not anyone supports our anti-Hindi struggle, we must conduct it. Hindi is opposed in Bengal too. Politically informed people, linguistic researchers, those with administrative experience, and distinguished experts all oppose Hindi.

“They can explain why and for what purpose they oppose it, but they cannot conduct the struggle.

“If the coming Hindi imperialism is to be defeated in Tamil Nadu, the struggle must be conducted continuously, without hesitation, successfully, without intrigue or deceit, and without allowing the heart to speak falsely. That is why the entire burden has been placed upon the DMK.

“We must fight against three kinds of programme. Congress autocracy is growing, and we must protect democracy. We bear the responsibility of replacing fifteen years of Congress rule, and the DMK has been carrying out that task. Opposing Congress’s political power, money power, and military power, we have secured fifty-one members of the Legislative Assembly and eight Members of Parliament. After achieving this, changing the Congress government appears very easy to us. Once such victories have been won, they may seem ordinary. When I see Nedunchezhian, Karunanidhi, and Anbil Dharmalingam, it does not even occur to me that they are MLAs. The Kazhagam raises its members of the Legislature and Parliament with that affection. In the Tiruchengode and Tiruvannamalai elections, we demonstrated that democracy could be developed and autocracy defeated.

“Second, we have a duty concerning the Anti-Secession Act. Third, there is the anti-Hindi agitation. How these three programmes are to be conducted will be announced at the conference in Madras. A plan for struggle may be prepared, but assembling the volunteers is the essential task. That is why we have set about explaining matters and gathering volunteers through conferences such as this.

“I did not attend the two training-camp meetings held in Salem and Thanjavur. Karunanidhi and our other Kazhagam comrades are preparing the volunteers.

“My work is to prepare the plan. We have therefore divided the labour into two halves and work separately. We decided in advance that many would conduct the struggle while many others would work for the party.

“I will not offer as an analogy the sight of a young squirrel climbing a large, mature tree, tasting a small fruit and dropping it below, where a large animal picks it up, tastes it, and eats it. I merely remembered an incident I once witnessed and mentioned it.

“Today Congress members describe precisely this as ‘resignation’ and ‘party work.’

“Speaking in the Rajya Sabha, Lal Bahadur Shastri said, ‘The decision to impose Hindi can no longer be changed. It has entered the Constitution. If you wish, ask us to introduce it gradually; ask us to introduce it without hardship. But it is impossible not to legislate Hindi as the official language.’

“We can expect nothing from them except these words, and we cannot oppose them in any other way.

“The victory we obtain by opposing Hindi will be a victory achieved by bringing down Congress autocracy.

“Victory in the anti-Hindi struggle will be like a mountain containing many medicinal herbs. It will equal a history containing many events.

“We have gathered today to bring these three issues to a victorious conclusion. Comrades, supporters, and members of the public from Thanjavur, Tiruchirappalli, and South Arcot districts must come forward and participate on a great scale in the forthcoming anti-Hindi struggle.”

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is preserved verbatim in the bilingual output. Clear contextual readings used only in English include “வன்சணாளர்கள்” as deceivers or agents of domination, “சனர்” as Chinese, “தி. மு. ௧.” as DMK, “இறந்த நிபுணர்கள்” as distinguished experts, and the missing initial characters in both occurrences of “கீழே” in the squirrel anecdote. “ஓம்பத்தொரு” is translated as fifty-one but remains an uncertain OCR/number reading. Political, linguistic, electoral, institutional, biographical, and historical assertions are translated in the source voice and have not been independently verified.
